---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Your Agent Is Already Paid For. Make It Count. | The Precon Market"
  description="Every pre-construction price already includes a commission for the buyer's agent. The question is whether it buys you a specialist who knows the project — or nothing at all."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[720px] px-4 pt-8 sm:px-6 sm:pt-12">
    <p class="section-label">— Representation</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold sm:text-4xl">
      Your agent is already priced into the deal. The question is what you get for it.
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/80">
      <p>
        Nearly every pre-construction project budgets a co-operating commission for the buyer's brokerage. It's baked
        into the price before you ever see it, and it gets spent whether you bring an agent or not. Walk in alone and
        it doesn't come back as a discount — the builder keeps it.
      </p>
      <p>
        But here's the part nobody says out loud: bringing <em>an</em> agent isn't the answer either. Most agents
        touch a pre-construction deal a handful of times in a career. Registered at the door, they're reading the same
        brochure you are, while a sales rep who does this every day controls the worksheet, the incentives, and the
        paper.
      </p>
      <p>
        That built-in commission should buy you someone who shows up already knowing the project: what the worksheet
        looks like this month versus last, which incentives are actually on the table, how the deposit structure
        compares to the building down the street, what the builder's past closings did to buyers on assignment
        restrictions, development-charge caps, and the HST rebate language this site's checker exists to catch.
      </p>
      <p class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 font-medium text-ink">
        Same price either way. Unrepresented, the commission is a gift to the builder. With a generalist, it's a
        formality. With a specialist, it's leverage.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card tick-corners mt-10 p-5 sm:p-8">
      <p class="section-label">— The offer</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold">Name the project. Get the real numbers.</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink/80">
        Tell me which project you're looking at — anywhere in the GTA — and I'll pull the current worksheet,
        incentives, and deposit structure before you set foot in the sales office. No cost, no commitment.
      </p>
      <form id="project-form" class="mt-5 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row">
        <label for="project-name" class="sr-only">Project name</label>
        <input
          id="project-name"
          type="text"
          placeholder="e.g. Sugar Wharf, Union City, Bristol Place…"
          autocomplete="off"
          class="min-h-12 flex-1 border border-rule bg-paper-pure px-4 text-base"
        />
        <button
          type="submit"
          class="inline-flex min-h-12 items-center justify-center border border-accent bg-accent px-6 font-medium text-white hover:bg-accent/90"
        >
          Get the numbers
        </button>
      </form>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink/50">Still deciding between projects? That works too — book the call anyway.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Carry the project name to /book so the headline can acknowledge it.
       Project name only — no personal data. -->
  <script>
    const form = document.getElementById('project-form') as HTMLFormElement;
    const input = document.getElementById('project-name') as HTMLInputElement;
    form?.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const project = input?.value.trim() ?? '';
      const params = new URLSearchParams({ source: 'project-intel' });
      if (project) params.set('project', project.slice(0, 80));
      window.location.href = `/book?${params.toString()}`;
    });
  </script>
</Base>
